import type { StoryTranslation } from "@/lib/stories";

/**
 * Traducciones al inglés, por id de historia. Lo que falte se toma del
 * original (por ejemplo, el texto de «Two beautiful bodies», que ya está en inglés).
 */
export const storiesEn: Record<string, StoryTranslation> = {
  "1": {
    title: "That Meadow Where We Lived",
    summary: "A man who no longer remembers his childhood is visited by a shadow that paints his memories blue.",
    description:
      "A stolen cigarette, a leafless tree and a white flower in a summer meadow. Years later, in the city, a shadow begins to visit him with paintings of his life and a brush soaked in the color of the sky.",
    content: [
      "Lately I have been trying to remember.",
      "I let waves of nostalgia break against my arid memory, hoping they will drench my mind in memories I can only dream of sinking into. Endless sleepless nights in which I keep my distance, watching thoughts that once were mine, begging to be swallowed by them, but they are no longer what they were, they are not what I felt; they are mirages of a life that no longer belongs to me. In idle hours I close my eyes and let myself be shipwrecked on the sea of memories in which I so long to drown. The weather is calm, the breeze is kind, and so I suffer, knowing my dream will never come true. I am a castaway sailing an ocean at peace, praying that in some fleeting moment the water will splash my parched sailor's face, and I will feel, at last, like a captain in a storm once more.",
      "The days pass at the speed of hours and I, lost in a world I never come to understand, do nothing but gaze at the garden of memories where, as a child, I used to lose myself. The sad petals, tired of waiting for my return, dry up, forcing me to forget what I once was. But between death and drought there is one flower that, against all odds, does not run dry, a memory that still radiates its young innocence. A memory to live for.",
      "I remember the blazing summer sun lighting the vast horizon, warm glimmers wrapping the beautiful world I came to belong to. Not a single cloud darkened the luminous show the sky put on that day, and the wind, to make amends for the drought of July, gently rocked the field around me. That afternoon, while my grandfather celebrated his birthday, I managed to steal a single cigarette and a lighter from the grown-ups' table, and I rode my bicycle to the meadow where I had once learned to tame it. I was seventeen when I sat down on the yellowed, withered grass of the field where I once lived my childhood, and leaned my back against the only tree in sight. Not a single leaf grew from it.",
      "That afternoon I chose to lean against a corpse, and for some reason it is the warmest memory I have.",
      "For hours I was an extension of those lifeless branches, serenely contemplating the cigarette, lifting it into the air, forcing my gaze to lose itself in that clean sky that always dressed in blue. All afternoon I could think of nothing but that simple color. How could thousands of people love a color so common? What is so special about wanting what everyone has? Everyone, my family included, fell in love with something that never once enchanted me. I asked whoever I could, trying to understand the hypnotic reality they all belonged to. What is it you see? Why do you fall in love with something that cannot see you? No one answered what I thought was an easy question; they couldn't believe I didn't feel what they felt, they couldn't imagine a life without the blue of the sky.",
      "In time, the summer began to die, and my curiosity with it. The last sunset before autumn came and, among the last rays of sun, I heard the only answer I would ever receive. “It's comforting,” my grandfather murmured, almost to himself, as he gazed at the bare horizon dying out before us, the two of us sitting on wooden chairs in the heart of the wide meadow. I remember looking at him after his simple answer, confused, and being answered only by the violet of nightfall reflected in his faded eyes.",
      "For long hours silence ruled the field, keeping company with the calm presence of the wind. I decided to stand up slowly, trying not to wake my dear old man, but his quiet voice caught me by surprise: “Do me a favor,” he recited, resting with his eyes closed. “Look in my room for a small book. Bring it to me when you find it.”",
      "Under his pillow I found a collection of photographs.",
      "He thanked me with his smiling eyes and bid me goodbye with a slight wave of his hands. As I left, I saw that man look tenderly at the book, but even from afar I saw his gaze tinged with sad thoughts I never managed to decipher. On that last dawn of summer, my grandfather never opened the book. He only held it close, as a good mother holds her frightened child, and chose to watch the lonely road along which we had all arrived, months before.",
      "—oo0oo—",
      "I took the first drag of my life staring at the dead grass on the ground. The dry smoke flooded my unsuspecting lungs and I could do nothing but cough, watering the withered grass with my bitter spit. I smiled, a slave to my new vice, unaware that on that day the sky had changed its color. Within minutes, the much-loved blue that had raised so many questions in me was smothered by a deep darkness. Thick clouds ruled the firmament, engendering an artificial night. Uneven gaps between them let the burning heat of the sun keep lighting the land. An image etched in me in ink: thin threads of light piercing colossal mountains of cloud, fighting to light the immense gloom my smoking had laid down. Soon the rain became part of the tragic scene, and so I decided to leave. I stood up carefully, trying not to hurt the old tree, thinking that with luck my company had inspired it to let new leaves grow. Once on my feet, I noticed a beam of light nearby. My curiosity made me visit it and, as I drew near, I could do little more than smile. At my feet, a white flower with frail petals, their edges signed in tiny golden ripples. I watched it dance faintly under the falling drops, like a ballerina, confused, unable to remember her next step on stage.",
      "I smoked my last drag gazing at the glorious play of lights that still keeps my sleepless nights lit. I lowered my head, crouched down, and rested what was left of the cigarette at the foot of the only flower in that pasture still brimming with young life, thinking the rain would put it out. That afternoon, as I left, I didn't look back. With every step, the clouds darkened my world, and it was between the gloom of the landscape and the crunch of my steps as I went that I saw, from the corner of my eye, the only flower that might have been still shining in the dark.",
      "—oo0oo—",
      "The world turned pale once I left the meadow where nothing shines anymore, the field that was lost among the clouds. Since that day, no color has woken a new obsession; no light has led me to a flower. I became a prisoner of monotonous streets where everyone lives in silence, afraid of waking from the loneliness in which they die. It is a sad world of the sick, and I, with a cigarette between my lips, was lucky enough to find the cure.",
      "Only a few months ago I left my apartment hoping to find the way out of this place. I took the pack of cigarettes and the lighter and wandered aimlessly through the labyrinthine streets everyone longs to escape, noticing the tall gray buildings where children grow up, pitying them for having nothing but cold metal stairs to play on. I spent a while looking at the great concrete structures that made up the metropolis where I smoked, but soon it began to rain. Unhurried, I threw the cigarette away and walked back home, thinking about the vice that had made me get soaked in that remote corner of the city. Why did I let it kill me? I couldn't understand how I was still hooked on those tasteless cigarettes if they no longer gave me the freedom of so many years ago.",
      "As I was thinking, a small family briefly caught my eye. From a distance I watched a mother try to shelter her son from the cold rain, hurrying from doorway to doorway, holding her little boy by the hand, but every now and then he would stop in the middle of the empty street, letting go of his mother to gaze intently at the warm color the streetlights gave off. The boy got soaked as he stared, restless, at what one day would no longer seem interesting at all. I could swear that in that moment I saw the streetlight shine a little brighter, trying to tuck its only admirer in against the cold around him. The mother went back to her son and he, without taking his eyes off his new obsession, lifted his arm to take her hand. They left as quickly as they had come, and once they were gone, the streetlights shone a little less. They had no reason left to shine; no one would ever look at them again. That day, for a very few seconds, I saw what few ever get to behold: the wonder of the ordinary being discovered once more.",
      "At last I reached my building. The cold had seeped through my clothes, pressing an unpleasant feeling into my skin. I went up to my flat shivering and, standing at my door, I realized it was open. As I slowly stepped inside, I saw a man, a silhouette standing before the only window of my small apartment. Anyone would have been terrified to see a shadow that was not their own, but for some reason I felt at peace. The stranger held a small canvas in his hand, half hidden by the long coat he wore, keeping me from seeing what he had painted. The shadow looked at me with empty eyes and, in a calm voice, asked me to sit. I took a small chair while he sat across from me in the armchair.",
      "He looked at me without a word, though his eyes spoke languages anyone could understand. There was a deep sadness in his dialect, with traces of weariness. After long seconds of calm, he slowly began to unveil the canvas.",
      "What I saw that afternoon stopped my world.",
      "The vividly colored image that man revealed was of the field I believed would never shine again. His art was a lost memory and, seeing it, I felt a warmth that only a pair of arms can give. I felt the cold and the gathered damp leave my body, leaving a gentle calm in my heart. I kept looking at the picture with incredible eagerness, overwhelmed to see again what I had lost so long ago. But between my tears and smiles, that impassive shadow watched me in pain. I looked him in the eyes, confused, as he took his brush out of a small pocket. I didn't understand what was happening, but he made sure to answer without words. From his great coat he drew a small pot of paint. A dark color, like the depths of the ocean. He began to dip the brush, delicately, into that so-beloved color. My confusion turned to terror. I couldn't believe I was about to lose what I had never managed to recover. I begged him, from the deepest part of my soul, to stop, not to do what he was about to do.",
      "“Let me keep it, please,” I sobbed with all the strength my frightened body could gather. “Why do you want to remember?” he replied in his sleepwalking voice. “I need something to live for,” I said, weeping, pleading with his soul to let me remember who I had been. The man stopped stirring the paint with his brush. He looked at me and began to ask about the details of the memory he held in his hands, but I could not answer a single question.",
      "The memory, though mine, was distant. Time had passed and the details were gone. For every question left unanswered, the man painted a blue line, thick and opaque, covering whatever color came before. The intensity of the memory faded into a blue that erased every feeling and every contour of what I had lived. Late in the afternoon, when the cold light of dusk came in through the window, the painting was a single color, and once again I was the owner of only one memory.",
      "The man kept coming, each time bringing memories I would kill to live again. On every visit he showed me paintings that summed up my life, but each time he questioned me, I suffered the stab of the brush once more. One day, the man drew a young woman. “Did you know her?” he asked in his unhurried voice. When I saw the drawing, my soul filled with joy. Yes, I remembered her! I told him everything, every tiny detail no one but me could know. I had to prove I remembered her; I wanted to prove she still mattered after all these years. Forgetting her was not my choice, it simply happened. I fought for her memory, I talked for minutes on end to save her from oblivion, but after everything I said he only asked, softly: “What was her favorite color?” I felt a deep weight in my chest, almost as if my heart had sunk. I thought I could defend her memory, but because of a question I never asked, there is now one less person I can remember. “I don't know,” I answered, and the man, with visible sadness, began to paint the canvas once more.",
      "In time, the visits grew less frequent; there were fewer and fewer memories left to fight for. On one of the last days he came, he showed me a painting. He asked me about it, but I didn't want to answer.",
      "“I never wanted to forget any of this,” I told him.",
      "He answered:",
      "“I know you didn't want to forget; no one ever does. But time heals everything, even what needs no healing. The way you look at the paintings gives you away. I know you treasure everything that once happened to you, and that you long to take those strange feelings by the hand again, though you no longer recognize them. But you must understand that what has happened to you is no coincidence. You chose to leave the countryside, your family. Not out of malice, but out of the hope of your young heart. You wanted to thrive and travel, to live the life you longed for so much. You packed your suitcase with hope, but who would have thought you'd forget to bring what you would need the most? You don't remember because nothing brings the memory back to you; you brought nothing that would help you look back. In that house in the meadow, the one I have painted hundreds of times, every corner overflowed with memories. Present and past blended into each other, making it hard to forget even the smallest story. You thought it would always be that way. But now that you live on a blank canvas, nothing gives back what you lost, nothing resembles the color of your home. Everything is new, but that is not a bad thing. Young as you still are, you can learn from what has happened to you. You had one great failing in your former life: you never asked. People vanish from your mind; you don't know who they were, the ones who tucked you in at night. A mother, a father, even a grandfather. You knew they were your family, but you never wanted to learn more. You stayed on the surface. You never asked about their favorite food, though they asked about yours; they knew everything about you, while you know nothing of the melodies that made them dance or the books that made them cry. Because you never asked about anything mundane, nothing in everyday life can carry you back. Had you asked about harmonies, you would remember your grandfather every time you heard a song; he loved them so. Had you asked about food, you would remember your mother's love of cooking; and about poems, your father, whenever you read any book about the Second World War. The only time you chose to ask was about that color you believed was so beloved. But you asked too late; I had asked already.”",
      "He rose on his way to the door:",
      "“I have no more memories to ask you about, no more paintings to take from you. It gives me no pleasure to know you have lost everything you loved so much, but you did little to avoid me. No one ever does.”",
      "After his farewell, the shadow of oblivion went away, leaving me to reflect on everything he had said on that first afternoon of spring. I never understood who that blue shadow was, but it is because of him that I now understand my family. They did not love blue; they simply accepted it as part of their lives. They all lost their battle to the vast color of the sky. “It's comforting,” my grandfather had said, speaking of his defeat. He no longer had to fight against forgetting. He chose to forget, for he had grown tired of defending his memories. My family surrendered, and what I took for their favorite color was the flag of their sad defeat before that shadow. It hurts to know they lost to oblivion, and that is why I will not concede it another victory. I will fight for my new memories, because now I understand that if, after a long life, memory is all we have left, we must make it easy to find. That is why that young boy will never forget the afternoon he watched the streetlights in the rain, for he will always see them from the window of his home; and that is why I will never stop smoking.",
      "After all these months of watching my memories disappear, I took it for granted that every moment I had lived before my thirtieth birthday, the day I met the man in blue, had been lost forever on an eternal canvas the color of the sky. But for some reason I don't understand, I was wrong: I am beginning to remember you. I see blond hair swaying in the field where I once grew up. I don't know what it is I see, I don't know who you are, but I know there was a time when you were everything to me. Your smile, your face, grow more present every day. I should be glad that I can remember you, but I am afraid of losing you again. I never asked you your favorite color and, for some reason, you are all that remains of who I was. In idle hours, I close my eyes and your memory bursts into my mind, adrenaline runs through my veins and I race through the house, terrified of forgetting you and giving the man in blue a reason to return. Frantic, I search for something to tie you to, but nothing is precious enough to remind me of you, so I have decided to keep you in the only place I know will never lose its worth, a place that will never drift away from me. Some time ago I wondered about my addiction to tobacco. I never knew why I couldn't quit, and only now do I understand that I am addicted to a past life, and that with every drag I am carried back to it. I have decided to shelter you in my mind, in the only memory that will not fade. I will always remember you as the flower that burned at the touch of my first cigarette; I will always remember you for as long as I am willing to let it kill me. I promise I will look for you in this new life and, if luck smiles on me, I will find you once again. For now I am learning to draw for this new adventure; I will not let myself forget anything that happens to me. When you see me, if you remember me, you will see how much I have changed since we were children, and I promise to draw in whatever you name your favorite color. I will remember you by what moves your soul, and you will remember me by my drawings, my art. The future is exciting; I am ready to begin this new chapter of my life, but I still don't know where to find you, and so I spend my days perfecting my drawings while I smoke, to be sure I'll have something to remember with. Only one thing is clear to me: I will keep smoking, I will smoke until we hold each other again. So that you and I, one way or another, in my memory or in the present, may meet once more in that forgotten meadow where we grew up hand in hand, so long ago.",
    ],
  },
  "2": {
    title: "Sands Without a Beach",
    summary: "Sitting on a darkening shore, someone lets the sand cover them while remembering a pair of black eyes.",
    description:
      "The beach fades beneath a closing sky and the sand begins to cover him. In the blackness of the landscape, a pair of eyes reappears in which part of him was lost forever.",
    content: [
      "I don't know how long ago I got lost. Sitting on the shore, a few meters from the sea, I watch the horizon stalked by a primal pain that forever defeats oblivion. I feel its strange thickness dwelling in the clouds, sliding over their exposed skins. Corrupting them. Forbidding even the most fragile gap to open between them. The white sky that once bloomed has become a harsh, grieving stroke of black that consumes the essence of what it was. The opaque firmament grazes the sea and the stars have turned immaterial. I can feel in my chest the new creation the sky is engendering. Something incomprehensible is about to be laid bare before my eyes. A coded essence, indifferent to me, forcing me to choose between the sameness of the gloom and the unsettling imminence of creation. The wind drags the charred sand of the landscape over my skin; it piles slowly upon my body, covering me, trying to sink me into its cold essence. The beach has grown tired of me. It does not want me in its new world. The sorrow of not being chosen spills into acceptance. I decide to keep watching what surrounds me. I hear the rattle of calm sliding over the gravel. It sounds like the beating of an arrhythmic clock. I think of the little time I have left on this beach on the edge of extinction. The sand keeps consuming me, but I don't move; I am in no hurry to flee my throne of coal. I watch the spent light of day sink into the ocean, losing itself in the ashen tide, never to return. The water wears the only color that bewitches my wounded soul. The waves, scraping against the rocks, mimic the song of sirens I never saw. I feel tired.",
      "The blackness of the landscape brings back the memory of her eclipsed marble eyes. I wonder what has become of her. I came to behold diamonds that were given the name of pupils; I made out in her eternal night the promise of day. The landscape reminds me of her name. Perhaps that is why I feel so calm letting myself be buried in the dark sand; to melt once more into her black eyes. To let my soul shed the weight it carries, to sink into her gaze and find myself again in the galaxy of her iris. To be once more that astronaut who got lost in her nebulous eyes. To visit secrets shaped like planets, to witness the sunsets of other worlds. She gave me a universe that part of me never wanted to leave. A fragment of me broke away to explore her fantasy. That essence of mine cut off all contact with me to lose itself in that beyond. Part of me became a wanderer in her glass eyes.",
      "From today on, the beach where I used to watch the stars, hoping to find myself, is clouded over. The horizon no longer shines as it used to; it tells the story of my bitter truth. Looking at the sky every night, dreaming of seeing my reflection in the stars, so that at last I might win myself back. But there are times I wake up thinking: what if I am the astronaut who never wanted to come back? What if this beach is her world, and she is beginning to forget me? I don't know if I am myself, or what I never could save. The sand has stopped consuming me. The wind blows the melody of loneliness. The tide rises, grazes my feet: it is urging me to swim. Tomorrow, when I wake, I will have left the sand. I don't want to see the new world that is about to form. Though I don't know if I'll come home looking at the sand, or swimming through that sea.",
    ],
  },
  "3": {
    title: "Seaweed",
    summary: "A poem about a girl the sea does not want to give back.",
    description:
      "Hair and clothes rocked by the tide, a girl who decided to play, and a silent war between the shore and the ocean to keep her memory.",
    content: [
      "Hair that once was smooth is rocked now by the tide.\nIt spreads like cracks through the cold realm where it floats.\nGray water mirroring the color of the sky:\na darkened gray, a bonfire's smoke.",
      "Long hours bled the color from her clothes.\nHer young skin, cracked open by the salt.\nFar off the waves are roaring; they weep.\nFor what she did: consequences none could have foretold.",
      "Her legs are sunk into a black abyss.\nRed eyes, though dead, keep watch on the depths of the sea.\nShe, only a little girl, who, smiling, chose to play.",
      "Hair and clothes, rocked by the tides, want to lead her back home.\nBut the current, ashamed, anchors the girl; it won't make her known.\nIt wants no one to find her, nor her black eyes to shine as once they shone.",
      "Between shore and ocean; a war.\n(All of them want her memory, all of them want her in their care.)",
    ],
  },
  "4": {
    title: "The Limit of a Poet",
    summary: "An artist who devoted his life to portraying someone discovers the limit was never the pencil.",
    description:
      "Pencils, ink, photography, glass and dance: no art can describe her. Applauded for a work he considers a failure, a poet decides to dedicate to her the only thing that will never cease to exist.",
    content: [
      "I always adored the idea of the sharpest pencil; it was an obsession that lingered in my head from the moment I aspired to be what I am. I told myself that with it I would portray the indescribable in detail, that drawing the intangible would be as easy as letting your voice inspire me. I spent countless nights dreaming, thinking I could at last draw the tiniest detail without a single flaw. I lived in a bubble of hope until I met your eyelashes; it was they that split my bubble of illusion. Something as simple as a pair of wavy lines terrified me; they looked nothing like the real thing. Any route to your eyes was inconceivable, thousands of strokes that, simple as they were, made me fail. I spent much of my life fixed on minuscule details, and still I could not even draw the hair I always loved. I tried your eyes, but I could never make them deep enough. Thousands of canvases and watercolors ruined, not one of them equal to the touch of your skin. I understood that the limit was the pencil, so I changed it.",
      "I tried to write an intimate ballad in blue ink, but the dictionary lacked the words, just as the musical scale lacked the notes. I tried to photograph you, but every picture ended up saturated by your radiance. Any image of you is like seeing a galaxy flooded with color. I sculpted your being in crystal, but never found glass that resembled your purity, and I created hundreds of choreographies that ended up an offense. There was no human movement that could describe your beauty.",
      "In time I realized that the limit of a poet is not his pencil, but his hands. For however much hope my eyes may shine with when I see you, it is my pulse that dictates the result. For thousands of years humans built cities out of mud. I don't understand how I could not draw you with the pencil that inspired me so. It was not a lack of will, it was a lack of skill. I am a dreadful artist and no one seems to notice what is wrong. After a whole life of work, I never got past the attempt, and the others applauded as if it were the final piece. They didn't realize the story was missing more. They don't know there is so much more to tell. They live in ignorance of my art, and it is a pain that will always stay with me. If the whole world burst into applause at my attempt to describe you, I cannot imagine how the universe would react to seeing you unveiled without a flaw.",
      "I am famous for my unrecognized defeat, a simple dictionary I made so I could define you; hundreds of words that don't exist, yet are needed to know you, words you engraved in the depths of my memory every time I saw you. I am not proud of reducing you to a handful of invented words, but that is all I could do for you. You are everything I could not draw: the twenty-seventh letter of the alphabet, the nonexistent musical note. That is why, however much the public loves it, I cannot dedicate the book to you; there would be no sense in dedicating to you the work I never managed to complete. It will always hurt that I could not create a new art for you, but that is my cross to bear. I cannot sign it, not with pride; I am not the artist everyone admires, I am a dreamer, a fraud who can never portray you. That is why I will not create again, for it is the ideas still floating in my mind that live in their fullest splendor, since they live in a place where no one tries to express them to anyone but me. I ask you to trust me, for I will never again declare my feelings, but I promise that if you look into the depths of my pupils you will see your reflection in colors that never came to be. I will dedicate to you what will never cease to live, for paper will rot with time and words will fly off on the wind. I dedicate to you what will always exist: my passion, my ideas, my love for you.",
      "The limit of a poet had a name, and it was yours.",
    ],
  },
  "5": {
    title: "The Uncertainty of Heat",
    summary: "A poem about a mirage in the desert.",
    description:
      "Between the burning sand and the refraction of the heat, a figure in the distance that seems to come closer and disappear, and a question in the cold night.",
    content: [
      "Between the stifling warmth and the dazzling glare of the setting sun\nWhile the orange sand cut me as the wind swept it along\nAnd my feet were buried in the ground\nI thought I saw you far away, watching.",
      "With barely any strength I dragged myself across the sand\nBanishing the gravel from my burned feet\nAnd through the refraction of the heat\nAnd the shadows of the dunes\nWith my tearful eyes, I seemed to see you see",
      "But as I took a beaten step,\nForcing myself to feel the burning of the wounds\nThat had already ceased to hurt,\nI looked at you in the distance\nHoping to see you near\nBut you seemed to disappear.",
      "I was tired,\nI lay down on the sand\nIt was no longer worth the climb",
      "That night, as the cold cradled me\nAnd the stars shone, fighting to stand out.\nI thought of that mirage, turning it over so that I could ask,",
      "Whether you're not here because you're drifting away\nOr because you were never here at all.",
    ],
  },
  "6": {
    title: "The Cold of the Blade",
    summary: "Death, weary of her work, sees a flower grow from her own tears.",
    description:
      "The Reaper walks barefoot through the meadow where she was born. From her tears sprouts a small orange flower she promises never to touch, until Winter whispers that it will come for it.",
    content: [
      "Death walked barefoot through the blue meadow where she was born. Unhurried, she moved through the dense grassland of her childhood which, in its immense beauty, lay steeped in a cool glow. It was the moon, companion of her girlhood, that lit the night, welcoming back the friend it had always adored. She wandered slowly through the memory of her home, sad tears flooding her eyes; for at last she understood that no one could be led by her hand to the only place where she had ever felt at peace. The wind tried to console her with a gentle breeze, tenderly rocking the meadow, trying softly to brush away her salty grief. She did not deserve to drown in sorrow. In the countryside, everyone knew the Reaper and her kind heart.",
      "That night, with grieving eyes, Death watched the faint shimmer of the stars, and confessed to the night that, by mistake, she had forbidden the wrong one from admiring the sky she had always loved. That evening, before she wept, the Reaper had visited a small house on the hillside. She never wanted to use her scythe when she worked; she knew it was a cruel way to say goodbye to life. She preferred to gently uncover the unfortunate soul and carry them in her arms to their new home, a place which, though perfect, no one wished to reach for fear of forgetting. That night, as she embraced a sweet old man who was talking in his sleep, she left her sterile weapon on the floor, unaware of a little grandson whose curiosity got the better of him. When she returned to the house for her tool, there was a tender, cold body clutching the instrument without strength, waiting to be taken back to his grandfather, so that he could tell him stories of the life he never got to live.",
      "Looking at the moon, Death asked whether her work did any good. She sobbed inconsolably, letting her acid tears water the dry mud; praying that from it would be born the little boy she had kept from growing. She slept surrounded by green grass, and in her dreams a mirage held her; it told her all would be well. The Reaper woke surrounded by dead grass; her friend, the moon, was gone. The universe delighted in reminding her of the curse of her being. She rose steeped in helplessness, looked at the pale grass and in that instant swore to the wind that no one would ever see her again. She would go far away, she would never hurt anyone again, nothing else would suffer at her skin.",
      "She began to walk, certain she would never return. But between bitter steps and thick sorrow, the wind blew hard, forcing her to look back at the meadow where she had learned to dream. And it was then that Death saw that from her tears a small orange flower was beginning to sprout. Overjoyed, she wanted to come close, to kneel and caress it, and welcome it to the world where the sun would shine on it all it could. But before her fingers grazed it, she remembered the curse of her touch and, from a distance, she gazed at that plant with delight, promising it she would never cause it pain.",
      "The months passed slowly, and from that young flower the field never stopped growing. The meadow that had always been green was robbed of its essence. Now it was flooded with colors that shone even in the darkest nightfall. Death, terrified of harming the plants, took a step back each time new ones sprouted; she was happy at a distance, knowing that, at last, something of hers took no one's joy away. When half the year had passed she decided to leave the field; her home was not hers, it belonged to what she would never stop loving.",
      "She went far away, explored the world, and never managed to find a flower like the one she had raised. She was happy, discovering, forgetting the work that, though hers, she had never wanted to do. For the first time she saw how people lived. They smiled. No one had the fear she was used to seeing. She decided that her absence was a reason for joy, and so she kept away from the crowds. Long weeks went by in which she withdrew from everything. And after hundreds of kilometers she finally sat down on the shore of an unknown beach to rest. She smiled as she looked at the ocean, remembering how her salty tears had made the miracle her touch could never achieve. She gazed at the horizon for hours; she lost her eyes in the distance, letting herself be rocked by the speech of the sea. Tired, she closed her eyes and dreamed once more. She let her mind guide her, but while she wasn't looking her clothes grew colder; the waves ceased to speak. It was then that she saw a black storm beginning to form. Death stood without taking her eyes off the horizon, and in that instant an icy whisper made itself known.",
      "“I will come for her,” Winter said to Death. “You won't be able to stop me.”",
      "The Reaper, terrified, ran back home to protect her flower. When she arrived, she saw a colossal field of flowers brimming with color. Each was of a different height and beauty, so that when the wind blew they seemed like waves of unimaginable pigmentation. Death, without coming near any of them, cried out for her creation, but none answered; they knew that Winter would follow her voice. The Reaper tried to help from a distance, but nothing could help them prepare for the start of this new season.",
      "In time Winter found the meadow, and with one slow touch the world that had been made there froze. Death watched flowers of intense color fade. She could not accept that this would happen to her daughter, and so she broke her promise never to come close, and went in.",
      "The cold grass crunched beneath her steps. The plants were brutally murdered by the frost. Death kept going, but she felt something terrible in her heart. It could not be that all this beauty should die in such a horrible condition. Where was the warmth of death? The last embrace before goodbye? The Reaper realized that everyone kills, but few do it with love. She could not let her daughter die alone in the cold, and so she decided to be the one who held her as she died, so she could whisper to her tenderly of a mother's love. She found her daughter at the center of the meadow, reminding her mother that she had been the heart that gave life to all that color. Her mother knelt with care, but her presence alone was curse enough; as she drew near, the flowers around her lost their strength, their petals grew weary, and the makeup of every one of them ran with fear. At last the Reaper covered her little girl with her body, forming a dome that shielded her from the hail. All winter long, Death held her only daughter. Not for a moment did she pull away. She told her the stories that, though she had wanted to, she had never told her; she spoke of her childhood in the meadow, and of the gift she never accepted. In time, Winter began to fade. The cold stopped freezing. Death rose carefully, only to see her daughter lying on the sand of that field where nothing would ever gleam again beneath the light of day.",
      "She remained seated, watching the end of the time she had been given to live. All the flowers lost their essence, their petals torn, their stems ripped from the ground by a wind heavy with malice. But among them all there was one that kept its color of happiness. She had been able to save her daughter from that unnamable, cruel death. The Reaper's family rested in peace.",
      "Death stood and, on her bare feet, began to walk away. She wept, hoping that in this new grief her daughter would bloom again. But nothing rose anymore; only the ice thawed, to make way for spring once more.",
    ],
  },
  "7": {
    title: "The Light Is at the Beginning of the Tunnel, Not the End",
    summary: "A tunnel we all walk through, moving away from the light we come from.",
    description:
      "On the long road we all choose as we grow up, those who turn back, and the certainty that, at some point, we will find the light again.",
    epigraph: "To be read with the song “Le tunnel” by Sylvain Chauveau",
    content: [
      "There is a tunnel we all walk into. They tell us there is no way out, and even so, we have never really cared. We all walk through it, forgetting the light we come from to explore the unnamed dark. Some grow tired, decide to turn around, and once they do, we never see them again. In the distance we see their silhouettes nearing the light we left behind, but the years go by and still, when we briefly look back, we see them walking on. It is a long road we have chosen, whichever way we wish to go. But a great adventure awaits us; that is why we walk, for our young eagerness to name. Our future awaits us, our eyes are shining, they guide us as we go. There is will and there is strength; we are lucky to have friends to walk beside. And though the light moves away from us, we know that it is where we're from, and someday we will find it again. We can always turn back, but for now our house is far away, and so we have accepted the road as our home.",
    ],
  },
  "8": {
    title: "In the Hallway of My House",
    summary: "Every morning, a window at the end of the hallway lets in a few seconds of dawn.",
    description:
      "The dust, the wood and the family portraits regain their color with the first light of day. Every morning he walks towards it, and every morning the sun hides again.",
    content: [
      "At the end of the decaying hallway of my house, there is a window that has managed to keep its youth. Through it the sun rises every morning, and for a few seconds, in one small instant, the honeyed clarity of day reveals the state of the corridor. I see the dust sail through the warm springs of dawn, rocked by imperceptible currents I only felt as I grew. The furniture regains its essence as the light floods in, showing once more the polished color of the seasoned wood it was made from, and the portraits of my family, in turn, reflect the golden gleam that springs from the far horizon. Every morning I walk toward the dawn, ignoring the crunch of glass beneath my feet, trying to come close to what is far and to understand the language in which that gilded spring whispers to me. But the flaw of the dawn is that it does not last. In a sigh, the gracious warmth blurs away; the sun hides among the artificial jungles of glass. Every morning I end up at the end of the hallway, consumed by the dark. Watching everything that gleamed go out, gazing at the half-open door of my room, remembering the fine brushstroke of morning caressing my face, understanding that no alarm will ever wake me before daybreak, realizing that I will only wake to the touch of the unreachable: the fleeting light of dawn.",
    ],
  },
  "9": {
    title: "The Theater",
    summary: "An entire audience waits in a ruined theater for the return of an old man who vanished behind the curtain.",
    description:
      "One November night, an old man steps onto the stage, confesses his life and disappears behind the curtain. The audience stays waiting for years, while outside the world goes on happening.",
    content: [
      "It crumbled at the mere touch of the wind, and yet your eyes never stopped watching it. Thousands of stories told between the peeling walls of the theater, delicately expressing the metaphorized condition of humankind. Its revenges, its hatreds; its longing to love. Floors infected with grimy green, seats corrupted by the absence of people. Shards that were windows once. Ancient carpets bathed in thick dust. Spotlights aimed at the shadows, trying to find in them the artists they were used to lighting. The actors' altar, forgotten behind the curtain of darkness.",
      "You were too young to remember, but one November day the audience vanished. Lost in the applause of the crowd, the actors believed they were the characters of their story. They gazed at the audience and, from the height of their glory, promised the audience that what they had witnessed that night was not a production. They offered the crowd the chance to explore a distant world; and an old man, worn down by time, raised his hand to be the tribute of the new performance. He made his way with slow steps to the center of the theater and, once the burning spotlights stung his pupils, the audience applauded. The man, exposed to all, was asked about his life, and, as he timidly clutched his worn jacket, trying to find shelter in it, he answered. He said that since childhood he had found no joy, that, while he waited for his death, he came to the theater to distract himself, to watch the lives that, though he had wanted to, he never lived.",
      "His voice echoed through the old theater, infecting the hearts of the crowd with a thick sadness. Long seconds devoured the happiness the previous act had cultivated; reality crept back in among the cold seats, reminding everyone of the frozen world that awaited them once they escaped the velvet walls that kept them from the winter. All of them, silent, watched the man slip into the great wall of fabric that separated fantasy from reality, and saw his body consumed by the supple curtain brimming with scarlet essence. As one, the audience reflected on the old man's existence, on the abyss his words had spoken, on the loneliness he had come to accept. A life worn down by the friction the shadows had worked upon his dreams. In the last stage of his adulthood, he chose to vanish before everyone to the other side of the theater, to see from a stage, with his tired eyes, the people he had never greeted for shame of being judged. His last look, cast at an audience of strangers, was a plea for forgiveness.",
      "—oo0oo—",
      "Sheltered in the absorbing magic of the theater, no one thought about the world beyond the glass. But that night, the grass froze. The night sky shone with a faint strength; the naked stars formed a new constellation. A white fox, walking through the withered wheat, found a tree without bark, wounded by the hail. It rested at the foot of its hollowed roots and, feeling the touch of its drooping branches, realized that by chance it had found the tree where its mother had raised it. It slipped into the frozen husk of its former life: a lifeless trunk, haunted by a soul that longed to rise again. It sank into it, and the warmth of its fur lent it new firmness. Trapped in the claws of winter, the tree blossomed. But, consumed by the spell of an empty curtain, no one in the theater saw. Waiting for the old man became the essence of the act. Hours dwindled into minutes. The world stood still. Everyone, from their seats, gazed at an empty stage, eagerly awaiting a new tale.",
      "But long years went by. Wrinkles deepened, eyelids began to grow heavy, and the last look of many was cast into the emptiness their expectations had created. In time, the sumptuous walls decayed. The hands of the clocks grew loose, stumbling in their pace. The building that had been a symbol of hope ended up eaten away by the years. Its roof, at last, caved in. And among the rubble of wood and cloth there remained a single person who, still from his seat, admired the night. From the ruins he gazed at the stars, letting the constellations tell him stories of the beyond. Weeks went by. By day he watched the empty act, and by night, the starry sky. The spring solstice came, bringing with it, on its first night, a fierce aurora borealis. He watched its blazing colors light up the sky, its brushstrokes across the black astral abyss.",
      "The man lost his senses amid so much beauty. He could not conceive that the world could express itself with such intensity. There was a universe out there, and he had shut himself away from such loveliness. With trembling strength he managed to rise from the dusty seat. And, in his intermittent madness, he decided to move on. He walked among the remains of the only building that had ever been able to speak, remembering the stories that, since he was a boy, had always inspired him to imagine. It was now, as a grown man, at the doors of the theater, that he understood his ailment. His mistake, like the old man's, had been to wait. His lips were no different from the actors'. His, too, could tell a tale.",
      "He looked one last time at the fallen curtain, through which the old man had escaped, and his hand pushed the door, carrying him into the world on which all stories are built. As he stepped out of the theater, spring was waiting for him with ardor. But among all the trees, only one remained frozen, awaiting the warmth of his touch to burst into color.",
    ],
  },
  "10": {
    title: "Goldie",
    summary: "Every night a boy plays on the beach, trying to make the Moon cry with his music.",
    description:
      "While his parents sleep, a boy slips away to the shore to play for the Moon. Without knowing it, his song awakens the memories and dreams of an entire coastal town.",
    content: [
      "On this warm summer night, musical notes sail across the ocean until they are lost at sea. A boy of remarkable skill plays his instrument, trying to see if his melody can make the Moon weep. His fingers gently caress the strings which, after so many days, he already knows how to make sing. His eyes lost in the starry abyss that is the sky, he has convinced himself that the worth of the art he has made can only be measured in tears. The calm night breeze joins the rhythm of the waves. Sand scattered by the gentle touch of the wind drifts like a faint mist barely an inch above the ground, reaching deep into the shy little town, where sleepless old men, high on their balconies, drink while their families stay asleep.",
      "It is always in the entrails of the night that the boy decides to slip away; his parents bolt the door of the room where they sleep, and, when he hears the metallic click from the hallway, he knows that he is free. He climbs out through his window and, after a short jump, the damp grass meets his small feet. Under the light of the stars he walks along the white-sand beach, watching, as he goes, how the shadows that live beneath the palm trees, swaying in the wind, begin to wave at him.",
      "Every night, on the shore of the town, where the sea comes in, the night calls up his song. And without knowing, while he looks only to the Moon, the world begins to change. In the town, thin cloth curtains sway, not with the breeze, but with his tune. The warm streetlights bloom with their faint glow, lighting the narrow streets where cats, hearing the melody, curl up to rest. Children who do not sleep begin, with open eyes, to dream; those old men who drink are returned to the memory they wanted to forget. A small coastal town that only shines in the dark. A lighthouse blinks in the distance, fitfully lighting the rooms where mothers without children can finally rest.",
      "Strangers united by a boy; everyone knows the reason for his song. And, though that reason is a sad one, it is when they hear it before dawn that the townspeople find their inspiration. The boy knows nothing of what his song can do; he only looks at the Moon, which answers him with indifference. But if he looked to the stars, he would see that it is they who, understanding his story, are crying, making a rain across the galaxy that everyone, astonished, cannot stop watching.",
      "There will come a day when the Moon is no longer his muse. And that day, however far away, will come.",
    ],
  },
  "11": {
    title: "Don’t ever change",
    summary: "In a meadow flooded by storms, a man asks a machine never to change.",
    description:
      "Everything around him transforms, sinks into the mud or disappears with time. Only the constant noise of a metal machine offers him the one certainty he has left.",
    content: [
      "His soul levitated with the relief of its voice: its perfect uniformity, its predictable function. The mud hungrily swallowed his worn-out shoes. The grass, greener than ever from the rain of unpredictable storms, lay scattered without harmony over that damp earth. Earthworms made their way through the slippery ground, eroded by the world's constant need to transform itself with time. The sky, patched with a shifting gray stain, kept changing color, revealing before his eyes an endless, lit-up wetland, only to cover it again. Trees in the distance poured springs from their deciduous leaves. The passing light of the sky swept the meadow in slow bursts, giving the calm sense of time slowing down. The wind rippled the half-drowned grass, whistling an inconsistent tune.",
      "Among the howls of the breeze, he heard its silver voice: its constant machining, cold gears grinding against each other without pause, making a single sound that inconsistent nature could never recreate. A cadenced melody of industrial percussion, shattering the unpredictable songs and silences of nature. Without coming closer, he could feel the inhuman touch of the apparatus. Its frigid, ferrous touch, polished until it became the smooth, continuous machinery it is. A metal obelisk designed to last, identical to the day it was made. Not a scratch nor a flaw, an immutable genius lost in a field drowned by storms.",
      "A world in constant change, with no promise of evolving. Everything ends up sunk by the rain, buried in mud and corroded by an unfeeling nature. He watches what was always there begin to slip through his fingers. The guarantee of the machine: to be the only thing made for a single reason, and to carry it out until it disappears. For years, the world has been shaped by the ticking of the minutes. Animals migrating in winter, never to come back. Fallen trees without a seed from which to grow again. A sky that, even at night, cannot promise stars. It is a world in motion, and he, from his window, watches it in astonishment, wondering whether it will be the same view when the sun decides to rise.",
      "Today the field is besieged by volatile storms. Uncontrollable gusts of rain that, with no pattern at all, pour down as hard as they can. The clearings of the plain are deceived, caressed at first by the water until they realize they are beginning to sink.",
      "In a world without certainties, he asks only that the machine never stop working. That it be whatever it is, and its machinery never grow old. That the vibrant sounds of compressed gas and rhythmic silences last until the meadow is no longer what it is.",
      "“Don’t ever change,” he whispered to the only certainty the man could have. He cried simple tears before leaving; the rain would not stop conquering. His weeping camouflaged by the endless metamorphosis of the storm. Giant puddles made of too much rain and too few tears anchor themselves in the mud to grow. The green grass that first drew him to that remote place can barely be seen.",
    ],
  },
  "12": {
    summary: "On the weight of what was never said, and two souls who find each other again beside a river.",
    description:
      "There is a void that grows for as long as it has no name. A man remembers the night when, at the end of a dark forest, he heard two souls reunite and understood why there are words that must never be left unsaid.",
  },
};
